<p>
  A standard multimeter measures voltage, current and resistance. An LCR meter measures inductance, capacitance and resistance. The Vartech V 8222 H does both from one handheld instrument - a genuinely useful combination for anyone who needs component-level LCR checks alongside routine electrical measurement, without carrying two separate instruments.
</p>

<h2 id="lcr-capability">What the LCR Function Actually Covers</h2>

<p>
  The V 8222 H measures inductance from 2 mH to 20 H across 2 auto ranges, with accuracy of ±(2.5% rdg + 30 digits), and capacitance from 20 nF to 2 mF across 6 auto ranges, with accuracy of ±(3.5% rdg + 20 digits). That's enough range to cover the great majority of discrete inductors and capacitors encountered in general electronics, motor-run and motor-start capacitor checks, and coil/transformer winding inductance verification - component-level LCR work rather than the tighter tolerances and multi-frequency testing a dedicated bench LCR meter provides.
</p>

<Callout type="tip" title="Auto-ranging on L and C">
  Inductance, capacitance and frequency ranging is automatic on the V 8222 H, while voltage, current and resistance ranging is manual - so when you switch to LCR measurement you don't need to guess a range first, the meter finds it. This matters in practice because component values in the field are rarely known precisely before you measure them.
</Callout>

<h2 id="full-multimeter-function">Full Multimeter Function on the Same Instrument</h2>

<p>
  Alongside LCR measurement, the V 8222 H is a complete True RMS digital multimeter: DC/AC voltage to 1000 V/750 V, DC/AC current to 20 A, resistance to 200 MΩ, frequency and duty cycle to 20 MHz across 7 auto ranges, and temperature from -20°C to 1000°C with a probe. True RMS measurement means accurate readings on the distorted waveforms found around VFDs and switch-mode loads - not just clean sine waves. Transistor (hFE) test, continuity and diode test round out the standard multimeter feature set.
</p>

<h2 id="who-this-suits">Who This Instrument Actually Suits</h2>

<p>
  The V 8222 H suits electronics technicians, service and repair work, and general maintenance where component-level LCR checks come up occasionally alongside routine electrical measurement - motor-run capacitor testing, coil continuity and inductance checks, and general component verification on a bench or in the field. If LCR measurement is your primary, daily task rather than an occasional check, a dedicated bench LCR meter with multiple test frequencies and tighter accuracy will serve you better - see our <a href={u('/blog/how-to-choose-a-vartech-lcr-meter/')}>Vartech LCR meter buying guide</a> for that comparison.
</p>

<h2 id="quick-reference">Quick Reference</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Occasional LCR checks alongside routine multimeter work</strong>: V 8222 H covers both from one instrument.</li>
  <li><strong>Motor-run/start capacitor and coil inductance checks</strong>: covered directly by the auto-ranging L/C function.</li>
  <li><strong>Daily, primary LCR measurement work</strong>: a dedicated bench LCR meter (LCR-1B, LCR-2A) offers multi-frequency testing and tighter accuracy.</li>
  <li><strong>General electrical measurement (V/A/Ω) with occasional temperature checks</strong>: fully covered by the same instrument.</li>
</ul>

